Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The evaluation trace shows the previous strategy successfully enforced SQL
// syntax, but accuracy collapsed because the generated query often degenerated
// into malformed identifier continuations such as `SELECT <id> .!!!!!` inside an
// otherwise parser-valid span. That indicates the issue is not delimiter control
// anymore; it is weak token selection inside the constrained SQL region.
//
// The revised strategy changes the constrained decoding policy:
// 1. We still emit exactly one constrained span and stop immediately after
//    closing it, preserving the successful 100% delimiter/syntax behavior.
// 2. Outside the span, we force `<<` immediately. Spider expects a single SQL
//    query, so there is no value in spending steps on free-form preamble.
// 3. Inside the span, we no longer rely on a generic constrained sample alone.
//    Instead, at each step we inspect whether the current SQL prefix is in a
//    narrow or low-branching state using parser queries. In those situations,
//    we deterministically bias toward a small set of parser-valid top
//    candidates, heavily penalize obvious punctuation drift (`!`, `.`, `,`,
//    `;`) when they are not parser-validated leaders, and then choose the
//    highest-logit valid candidate. This directly targets the observed runaway
//    punctuation/id-tail failures.
// 4. When the parser says the SQL prefix is complete, we immediately close with
//    `>>` and terminate. EOS remains terminal in every branch.
//
// The core change is therefore a stronger, parser-aware token choice policy
// inside the constrained span, while keeping the proven one-query control flow.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: Outside the constrained span, the implication is vacuous.
// Opening the span uses OpenConstrainedSpan, which yields an empty constrained
// prefix, and parser.IsValidPrefix([]) is a precondition. In the active span,
// the "complete" branch closes the span and makes the implication vacuous; the
// EOS branch breaks without changing state; and every token-extension branch
// updates state only through AppendConstrainedToken with a token obtained from
// TopValidCandidates or ConstrainedStep, so the new constrained prefix remains
// parser-valid.
// suffix: Outside the span and after closing it, the implication is vacuous.
// Immediately after opening, currentConstrainedOut is [], so the suffix claim is
// trivial. In every append branch, AppendConstrainedToken appends the chosen
// token both to generated and to currentConstrainedOut, preserving that the
// constrained prefix is exactly the suffix of generated. EOS break leaves state
// unchanged.
// cost accounting: steps is the returned cost measure. Each loop iteration that
// mutates or decides generation performs exactly one costed helper call:
// OpenConstrainedSpan, ConstrainedStep, or CloseConstrainedSpan. Query helpers
// such as TopValidCandidates, DeadEndDetection, ValidTokenCount, and logit
// shaping helpers do not affect returned cost. We increment steps exactly once
// in each such outer iteration, and break branches do not increment it again, so
// cost := steps at exit and thus cost <= maxSteps.
// progress: Each outer iteration increases steps by exactly 1 before any next
// iteration. Opening the span adds one token. Inside the constrained span,
// CloseConstrainedSpan adds one token, AppendConstrainedToken adds one token,
// and an EOS break adds none. Therefore each iteration increases |generated| by
// at most 1, which is bounded by the fresh slack of stepTokenBudget >= 1 added
// when steps increments, so
// |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps * stepTokenBudget is preserved.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

var steps := 0;
var openedAnySpan := insideConstrained;

while steps < maxSteps
  invariant 0 <= steps <= maxSteps
  invariant lm.ValidTokensIdsLogits()
